    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Originalists heavily rely on the specific wording of the Second Amendment, pointing out that the Second Clause containing \"to the people\" modifies the first Clause's restriction on standing armies, rather than serving as a separate justification for a collective right. They argue that the historical context of the 1790s reveals that \"common menengahers\" and citizens exercising self-defense were essential for maintaining security against tyrannical governments, leading figures like Judge Stephen Harris and legal scholars to assert that individual warrantless arms possession was a pre-existing right protected by the Eighth Amendment and individual liberty. Furthermore, they utilize historical records such as *obiter dicta* from Chief Justice John Marshall's opinion in *United States v. The Washington*, which debated the separate recital of state militias, to demonstrate that the framers viewed the right to keep and bear arms as inherent and critical to civilian life, not merely a functional requirement of the military. These textual and historical data points form the bedrock of the argument that the Amendment protects an individual right unrelated to militia service.",
      "source": "target",
